 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "s1",
   "text": "Marvel burned a mutant down to his bare skeleton, then let him grow back. Fans called it the most ridiculous healing ever drawn.",
   "hook": true,
   "vis": {
    "t": "kb",
    "src": "regrow_skeleton2.jpg",
    "z0": 1.1,
    "z1": 1.26,
    "cx": 0.5,
    "cy": 0.45
   },
   "gap": 0.18
  },
  {
   "id": "s2",
   "text": "2006. Wolverine issue 43. A villain called Nitro detonates at full power, and strips every ounce of flesh off Logan's bones.",
   "vis": {
    "t": "kb",
    "src": "regrow_blast.jpg",
    "z0": 1.24,
    "z1": 1.1,
    "cx": 0.5,
    "cy": 0.45
   },
   "gap": 0.15
  },
  {
   "id": "s3",
   "text": "A few pages later, he's back. Muscle, skin, the lot. The usual explanation? His healing lives in his bone marrow, sealed inside metal nothing can break.",
   "vis": {
    "t": "kb",
    "src": "regrow_halfhealed2.jpg",
    "z0": 1.0,
    "z1": 1.12,
    "cx": 0.42,
    "cy": 0.4
   },
   "gap": 0.15
  },
  {
   "id": "s4",
   "text": "Except nature already does something wilder. This is a planarian. A flatworm about the length of a fingernail.",
   "vis": {
    "t": "vid",
    "src": "regrow_clip_glide.mp4",
    "ss": 0,
    "fit": "square"
   },
   "gap": 0.15
  },
  {
   "id": "s5a",
   "text": "In 1898, biologist Thomas Hunt Morgan cut them into pieces.",
   "vis": {
    "t": "kb",
    "src": "regrow_morgan_fig4.jpg",
    "z0": 1.0,
    "z1": 1.12,
    "cx": 0.5,
    "cy": 0.52
   },
   "gap": 0.1
  },
  {
   "id": "s5b",
   "text": "A fragment just one two hundred and seventy ninth of the worm grew back into a whole new animal.",
   "vis": {
    "t": "kb",
    "src": "regrow_morgan_fig41.jpg",
    "z0": 1.0,
    "z1": 1.1,
    "cx": 0.5,
    "cy": 0.52
   },
   "hero": {
    "at": 3,
    "lines": [
     "1/279",
     "OF A WORM"
    ],
    "col": "a2",
    "y": 390,
    "size": 140
   },
   "gap": 0.1
  },
  {
   "id": "s5c",
   "text": "Head, brain, gut, everything.",
   "vis": {
    "t": "vid",
    "src": "regrow_clip_head.mp4",
    "ss": 0,
    "fit": "square"
   },
   "gap": 0.15
  },
  {
   "id": "s6a",
   "text": "In 2011, scientists went further. They took a worm whose own stem cells had been wiped out by radiation,",
   "vis": {
    "t": "kb",
    "src": "regrow_wild.jpg",
    "z0": 1.05,
    "z1": 1.3,
    "cx": 0.62,
    "cy": 0.55
   },
   "gap": 0.05
  },
  {
   "id": "s6b",
   "text": "and gave it one single cell from another worm. That one cell rebuilt the entire body.",
   "vis": {
    "t": "vid",
    "src": "regrow_clip_regrown.mp4",
    "ss": 0,
    "fit": "square"
   },
   "gap": 0.15
  },
  {
   "id": "s7a",
   "text": "Wolverine needed his whole skeleton to come back.",
   "vis": {
    "t": "kb",
    "src": "regrow_skeleton2.jpg",
    "z0": 1.36,
    "z1": 1.16,
    "cx": 0.5,
    "cy": 0.4
   },
   "gap": 0.1
  },
  {
   "id": "s7b",
   "text": "The flatworm needs one cell.",
   "vis": {
    "t": "vid",
    "src": "regrow_clip_glide.mp4",
    "ss": 0,
    "fit": "square"
   },
   "gap": 0.3
  }
 ]
}
